00:06:33.000 --> 00:07:00.000
that he admired and showed them his portfolio. They all said no. But if they smiled when they said no, then he went back the next day and kept knocking. And finally he had two offers, one for $3 a week, maybe five, and one for nothing. But he took the job that was for nothing because he thought he would learn more and be in a better position to advance. And after the first week they started paying him.
那些他十分敬佩的建筑师，并向他们展示了自己的作品集。他们都拒绝了。但如果他们在说“不”时面带微笑，他第二天就会再去，继续敲门。最后他得到了两份工作邀请：一份是每周3美元，也许是5美元；另一份则是零报酬。但他接受了那份零报酬的工作，因为他认为这样能学到更多东西，并能处于更有利的发展地位。而在第一周过后，他们开始付给他薪水了。

16
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:30.000
Paul Williams worked for a landscape architect, a residential architect, and a commercial architect before he was licensed to practice in California in 1921. The following year, he started his own firm, Paul R. Williams and Associates. Williams's first solo commission was a house for a well-connected high school friend, and his neighborhoods in Los Angeles blossomed word about Williams' spread. In the 20s or early 30s, if you go to dinner at somebody's house, because they have a new house, they say, who's your architect? There's one more, one more, one more.
保罗·威廉姆斯先后为一位景观建筑师、一位住宅建筑师和一位商业建筑师工作过，之后于1921年获得了在加利福尼亚州独立执业的资格。第二年，他创办了自己的事务所——保罗·R·威廉姆斯联合事务所（Paul R. Williams and Associates）。威廉姆斯接手的第一个独立委托项目是为一位人脉广泛的高中同学设计一栋房子，随着他在洛杉矶的社区里名声大噪，关于威廉姆斯的传言也开始流传开来。在20世纪20年代或30年代初，如果你去别人家吃晚饭，因为他们搬了新家，他们就会问：“你的建筑师是谁？”一个接一个，源源不断。

17
00:07:30.000 --> 00:08:03.000
Williams had an impeccable sense of scale. And he knew just how to situate a structure on a property to make best use of beautiful views and sunlight. He helped create what would come to be known as the Hollywood style, that opulent mixture of Mediterranean, European, and colonial influences, with swimming pools and sweeping staircases. It's a rich and sumptuous sensibility, but Paul Williams kept these mentions very clean and classy. Paul R. Williams and associates grew quickly and became renowned.
威廉姆斯对空间尺度有着无可挑剔的敏锐感知。他非常清楚如何在宅基地上布局建筑，从而最大限度地利用美丽的景观和阳光。他协助开创了后来被称为“好莱坞风格”的建筑流派——那是地中海风情、欧洲风情和殖民地风情的奢华融合，往往配有游泳池和气势宏大的楼梯。这是一种丰富而华丽的审美品位，但保罗·威廉姆斯却能将这些豪宅设计得极为清爽高雅。保罗·R·威廉姆斯联合事务所迅速发展壮大，并声名远播。

18
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:26.000
Although some clients would be taken aback when they met Williams. Because you know they may have read about him, you know, may have heard about him, but they didn't realize he was black. He didn't want people to be uncomfortable. And often they were. I mean it's one thing to hire somebody, it's one thing to think that he's the best around and you want the best around. It's another thing to think that he's the best around and you want the best around. It's another thing to actually touch him.
尽管有些客户在见到威廉姆斯时会感到大吃一惊。因为你知道的，他们可能阅读过关于他的报道，可能听说过他，但他们并没有意识到他是个黑人。他不想让人感到尴尬。而他们往往确实感到尴尬。我的意思是，雇佣某人是一回事，认为他是周围最优秀的、并且你想要最好的是一回事。而真正去触碰他，又是另一回事。

19
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:55.000
Many of his white clients had never interacted with a black professional before. Williams had to find ways to work around white people's discomfort with him. He would not put people in a position where they felt like they had to shake his hand if they didn't want to. So he would put his hands at his pockets or behind his back. And Williams also recognized that many clients wouldn't want to take a seat next to him. And so to make them feel comfortable and to possibly persuade people who weren't sure
他的许多白人客户此前从未与黑人专业人士打过交道。威廉姆斯不得不寻找方法来缓解白人面对他时的不适感。他不会把人置于一种“如果他们不想的话，却不得不跟他握手”的境地。因此，他会把双手插在口袋里或背在身后。威廉姆斯还意识到，许多客户可能不愿意坐在他身旁。为了让他们感到舒适，并尽可能说服那些对

