00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:09.000
about having this black man, he could sit on one side of the desk, you'd be on the other side. And he would ask you, you know, do you want a formal living room, do you want a formal dining room, do you want a din because you have little kids, do you want this, do you want a formal living room? Do you want a formal dining room? Do you want a den? Because you have little kids. Do you want this? Do you want that?
雇用这位黑人男子心存犹豫的人，他可以坐在办公桌的一侧，而你坐在另一侧。他会问你，你知道的：“你想要正式的起居室吗？你想要正式的餐厅吗？因为你有小孩，你想要一个休闲室吗？你想要这个吗？你想要正式的起居室吗？你想要正式的餐厅吗？你想要休闲室吗？因为你有小孩。你想要这个吗？你想要那个吗？”

21
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:30.000
And then facing the client opposite the table, Paul Williams would sketch out a vision of the house, upside down, so that it faced the client. And he would sketch it upside down and come alive before your eyes. And I never interviewed anyone who saw him do it, who did not just light up as soon as we talked about it. It was like you should have seen him.
然后，坐在桌子对面面对着客户，保罗·威廉姆斯会勾勒出房子的设计构想，而且是倒着画的，这样图案正好面向客户。他会倒着把图画出来，让设计在你的眼前生动呈现。我从未采访过任何看过他这样作画的人，在谈到这个话题时他们的眼睛不是立刻放光的。那感觉就像是：“你真该亲眼看看他当时的样子。”

22
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:58.000
You've heard the expression ginger Rogers did everything for the stared at except backwards and in high heels. Well Paul Williams did everything his white peers did except upside down and better. Williams was not the first architect to draw upside down, but it's indicative of the lengths he went to accommodate his white clients. He always dressed impeccably and worked tirelessly. He once wrote about a meeting with a potential client who warned
你听过这样的说法：金格·罗杰斯（Ginger Rogers）完成了弗雷德·阿斯泰尔所做的一切，只是她是倒着跳的，而且还穿着高跟鞋。嗯，保罗·威廉姆斯完成了他的白人同行们所做的一切，只是他是倒着画的，而且做得更好。威廉姆斯并不是第一个倒着画图的建筑师，但这表明了他为了迁就白人客户而做出的巨大努力。他总是着装无可挑剔，工作不知疲倦。他曾在一篇手记中写到与一位潜在客户的会面，对方警告

23
00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:29.000
Williams that he was also speaking with a number of other architects. The potential client asked how soon Williams could submit preliminary drawings. Four o'clock tomorrow afternoon said Williams. The client thought it was impossible because all the other architects had asked for two or three weeks. And you can bet that yes, Paul Williams delivered that plan at four o'clock the next day as promised. But he didn't tell the client that he had worked for 22 hours straight without eating or sleeping. He felt like he had to be light years better just to be accepted.
威廉姆斯说，他当时也在与许多其他建筑师交谈。潜在客户问威廉姆斯多久能交出初步图纸。“明天下午四点，”威廉姆斯说。客户认为这不可能，因为其他所有建筑师都要求两到三周。你可以打赌，没错，保罗·威廉姆斯正如承诺的那样，在第二天下午四点交出了那份图纸。但他没有告诉客户，他已经连续工作了22个小时，没有吃饭也没有睡觉。他觉得为了被接受，自己必须比别人优秀不知多少倍。

24
00:10:29.000 --> 00:11:05.000
And, you know, often he wasn't accepted. Sometimes Williams was unwelcome in the very places he was designing. And even after he had earned the respect of his clients, he had to tolerate mistreatment from subcontractors and painters and plumbers. You know, you hire him to do your home and then some of the sub people who are working on the home are like, I'm not taking orders from this black guy. It's a regular kind of thing. Throughout his career, even as he designed mansions for wealthy clients, Williams also designed affordable homes for middle-class Angelinos.
而且，你知道的，很多时候他并没有被接受。有时，威廉姆斯在他所设计的那些地方并不受欢迎。甚至在他赢得客户的尊重之后，他还得忍受来自分包商、油漆工和水管工的虐待。你知道的，你雇他来设计你的家，然后一些在家里工作的分包人员却说：“我不会听从这个黑人家伙的命令。”这是一种常见的情况。在他的整个职业生涯中，甚至在为富裕客户设计豪宅的同时，威廉姆斯也为中产阶级的洛杉矶人设计经济适用房。

